WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.028 --> 00:00:24.209
Cosette looked at Jean Valjean holding out the doll to her. Then she ran and hid as far as she could under the table.

2
00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:30.114
No one in the room moved or spoke. Only Monsieur Thenardier dared to say anything.

3
00:00:30.115 --> 00:00:34.842
"That doll must have cost at least thirty francs," he whispered to his wife.

4
00:00:34.843 --> 00:00:40.082
"This fellow obviously has money, so it's time to turn on the charm."

5
00:00:40.083 --> 00:00:49.598
"My dear Cosette," said Monsieur Thenardier. "This kind man has given you a present. Please come out and accept it."

6
00:00:49.599 --> 00:00:56.328
Cosette crawled out from under the table and gazed at the beautiful doll with both terror and hope.

7
00:00:56.329 --> 00:01:03.336
She worried that if she touched it, the Thenardiers would scold and beat her. But it was so beautiful . . .

8
00:01:03.337 --> 00:01:07.415
"Is it true?" Cosette burst out. "Is the doll for me?"

9
00:01:07.416 --> 00:01:15.899
For a moment Valjean could not speak for fear of weeping. He nodded to Cosette and put the doll's hand in hers.

10
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:22.154
"I'll call her Catharine," said Cosette, hugging the doll. "May I sit her in a chair, madame?"

11
00:01:22.155 --> 00:01:31.716
"Yes, my child," said Madame Thenardier. Her voice was as sweet as honey, but her eyes were full of rage.

12
00:01:31.717 --> 00:01:36.820
Cosette placed the doll on a chair, and then sat on the floor admiring it.

13
00:01:36.821 --> 00:01:40.470
"Why don't you play with your new doll?" asked Valjean.

14
00:01:40.471 --> 00:01:45.807
"Oh, I am playing!" said Cosette without taking her eyes from the doll.

15
00:01:45.808 --> 00:01:49.137
Valjean had to turn away and wipe his eyes.

16
00:01:49.138 --> 00:01:55.341
Madame Thenardier whispered to her husband, "It's outrageous that Cosette has such a nice doll.

17
00:01:55.342 --> 00:01:59.033
Tomorrow I'm throwing that child out on the street."

18
00:01:59.034 --> 00:02:03.208
The next morning before dawn, Valjean came into the kitchen.

19
00:02:03.209 --> 00:02:10.359
"Ah, monsieur," said Madame Thenardier. "Are you leaving us today?"

20
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:12.298
"Yes, I must be going."

21
00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:15.097
She handed him the bill for his stay.

22
00:02:15.098 --> 00:02:24.922
"I'm sorry we have to charge so much, but we're just getting by. I have my own girls, and then there's this other girl we've taken in."

23
00:02:24.923 --> 00:02:32.351
Valjean did not want to betray too much interest in Cosette. "Suppose I were to take her off your hands?"

24
00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:39.573
Madame Thenardier could not believe her good fortune. "I must fetch my husband, so you can discuss it with him."

25
00:02:39.574 --> 00:02:44.668
After a whispered conversation with his wife, Monsieur Thenardier came into the room.

26
00:02:44.669 --> 00:02:54.661
"I hear you want to take our little Cosette away from us. I'm sorry; I cannot consent to that. We've grown so attached to her.

27
00:02:54.662 --> 00:03:01.645
It's true that she costs us money and she has her faults, but I can't just give her away to a stranger."

28
00:03:01.646 --> 00:03:09.217
Valjean looked steadily at Thenardier as the innkeeper continued to pretend that he was devoted to Cosette.

29
00:03:09.218 --> 00:03:15.376
"If you took her away," said Thenardier, "I'd want to know your name and where you live.

30
00:03:15.377 --> 00:03:21.717
Then I could come visit her, and she'd know that her good foster father was still watching over her."

31
00:03:21.718 --> 00:03:27.187
"Monsieur Thenardier, if I take Cosette, you'll never know my name or my address.

32
00:03:27.188 --> 00:03:33.288
My intention is that she'll never see you again in her lifetime. Do you agree to this or not?"

33
00:03:33.289 --> 00:03:37.476
The innkeeper did not respond. He had noticed something.

34
00:03:37.478 --> 00:03:43.526
Even before the stranger had proposed taking Cosette, the man had kept looking at her the previous night.

35
00:03:43.527 --> 00:03:52.296
Who was this man and why was he so interested in Cosette? He was too old to be her father, but could he be her grandfather?

36
00:03:52.297 --> 00:04:00.098
And why did he have so much money when he was dressed so poorly? Thenardier decided that now was the moment to act boldly.

37
00:04:00.099 --> 00:04:07.526
"Monsieur, if you want Cosette, then you must give me fifteen hundred francs."

38
00:04:07.527 --> 00:04:16.010
Valjean opened his wallet, pulled out three five-hundred-franc bills, and laid them on the table. "Bring Cosette."

39
00:04:16.011 --> 00:04:20.831
When Cosette came into the kitchen, she found Valjean untying his bundle.

40
00:04:20.832 --> 00:04:29.835
He presented her with a warm dress, apron, undergarments, petticoat, scarf, wool stockings, and shoes.

41
00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:33.987
All of the clothing was black because her mother was dead.

42
00:04:33.988 --> 00:04:38.668
As dawn broke, Valjean walked away from the Thenardiers' inn.

43
00:04:38.669 --> 00:04:45.057
He was leading a little girl in mourning clothes who was carrying a large doll dressed in pink.

44
00:04:45.058 --> 00:04:51.261
Cosette did not know the man who had taken her away from that horrid life, but she trusted him.

45
00:04:51.262 --> 00:04:57.390
She felt that she was no longer alone and that she had someone who would protect her.

46
00:04:57.391 --> 00:05:03.628
Soon after Cosette had left the inn, Thenardier showed the fifteen hundred francs to his wife.

47
00:05:03.629 --> 00:05:12.620
"You fool," she said. "Is that all you got for the girl when that man was carrying so much money in his wallet?"

48
00:05:12.621 --> 00:05:18.863
"You're right. I should have asked for more. He probably would have given me fifteen thousand francs."

49
00:05:18.864 --> 00:05:23.120
Monsieur Thenardier put on his hat. "I'm going after them."

50
00:05:23.121 --> 00:05:28.527
By the time Thenardier left the inn, Valjean and Cosette had a good head start.

51
00:05:28.528 --> 00:05:35.445
But a man in pursuit walks quickly, and a child walks slowly and needs to rest often.

52
00:05:35.446 --> 00:05:39.774
Thenardier caught up to them when they were sitting by a path near some woods.

53
00:05:39.775 --> 00:05:44.509
His walk had given him just enough time to formulate a scheme.

54
00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:50.922
"Pardon me, monsieur, here are your fifteen hundred francs," Thenardier said to Valjean.

55
00:05:50.923 --> 00:05:52.196
"What do you mean?"

56
00:05:52.197 --> 00:05:55.763
"I mean that I'm taking back Cosette."

57
00:05:55.764 --> 00:05:59.381
The little girl shuddered and huddled close to Valjean.

58
00:05:59.382 --> 00:06:07.668
"You're taking back Cosette?" Valjean said slowly as he looked Thenardier in the eye.

59
00:06:07.669 --> 00:06:15.073
"I've thought it over, and she really belongs with her mother. Now you'll say that her mother is dead.

60
00:06:15.074 --> 00:06:23.573
In that case I can only give her to someone who brings me a written order from the mother, saying that I should deliver Cosette to him."

61
00:06:23.574 --> 00:06:29.882
Valjean reached into a pocket and produced the letter that Fantine had signed before she died.

62
00:06:29.883 --> 00:06:37.882
"You're completely correct," he said, "but I think this letter will settle the matter. You're familiar with the signature?"

63
00:06:37.883 --> 00:06:42.453
Thenardier read the letter and instantly recognized Fantine's signature.

64
00:06:42.454 --> 00:06:49.382
Defeated, he decided to make one last desperate attempt to get money from Valjean.

65
00:06:49.383 --> 00:06:56.033
"It says here that the bearer of this letter will pay all the money that Fantine owes me.

66
00:06:56.034 --> 00:07:03.374
It will take me a while to calculate exactly how much that is, but I know it's quite a large amount."

67
00:07:03.375 --> 00:07:10.779
Valjean stood up and towered over Thenardier. "Now you listen to me," he said in a menacing voice.

68
00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:17.517
"I know exactly how much money Fantine owed you, and I know that she paid you much more than that."

69
00:07:17.518 --> 00:07:24.089
But Thenardier was not intimidated. "I'll take Cosette back if you don't give me three thousand francs."

70
00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:32.747
Valjean said quietly, "Come, Cosette." He took her hand in his left hand, and picked up his walking stick with his right hand.

71
00:07:32.748 --> 00:07:39.381
Thenardier noted the enormous size of the heavy stick and decided not to say anything more.

72
00:07:39.382 --> 00:07:43.336
He watched as Valjean disappeared into the woods with Cosette.

73
00:07:43.337 --> 00:07:48.320
"I'm going to keep an eye on them," he decided after a few minutes.

74
00:07:48.321 --> 00:07:54.145
He had followed the pair for two or three hundred feet before Valjean turned around and glared at him.

75
00:07:54.146 --> 00:08:02.075
Valjean's look was so threatening that Thenardier decided to abandon them and go home.
